This PR is a tidy up of the `UpdateReleaseFilesPublishedDate` spotted after making changes in #6694.

Previously the method looked like this:

```csharp
  private async Task UpdateReleaseFilePublishedDate(ReleaseVersion releaseVersion, DateTimeOffset publishedDate)
  {
      var dataReleaseFiles = contentDbContext
          .ReleaseFiles.Where(releaseFile => releaseFile.ReleaseVersionId == releaseVersion.Id)
          .Include(rf => rf.File);

      if (releaseVersion.PreviousVersion is null)
      {
          await dataReleaseFiles.ForEachAsync(releaseFile => releaseFile.Published = publishedDate.UtcDateTime);
      }
      else
      {
          await dataReleaseFiles
              .Where(rf => rf.Published == null)
              .ForEachAsync(releaseFile => releaseFile.Published = DateTime.UtcNow);
      }
  }
```

This appeared to have a few different problems:
- `releaseVersion.PreviousVersion is null` looked unsafe because property `PreviousVersion` is not guaranteed to be loaded, and if it's not loaded this would always evaluate to true even if a previous version exists. It would have been safer to use `releaseVersion.Version == 0`. The calling method `CompletePublishing` fetches the release version, but also does not have an explicit `.Include` on `PreviousVersion`. I believe this was only working by coincidence because the method `CalculatePublishedDisplayDate` called in the interim loads it. This seemed potentially quite dangerous.
- `.Include(rf => rf.File)` looked unnecessary.
- When `releaseVersion.PreviousVersion is null` is true, i.e. because it's the first version, all of the release files are new, so they will have no value of `Published`. So both branches could have been identical, provided that we want to set the same value for `Published`, which I think we do...
- Using `DateTime.UtcNow` meant that all files get a slightly different `Published` time which is also slightly different to the date the release actually gets published.
- The `publishedDate` parameter being passed into this method was the published display date which is the inherited or overridden published date calculated for amendment versions dependent on whether the box 'Update publish date' is ticked on approval. However the method was only setting this date if `releaseVersion.PreviousVersion is null`, i.e. because it's the first version. For first versions the published display date is always the same as the actual published date because there's no previous version for it to inherit from. So regardless of what the version number is, I think we always want to use the actual published date for the value of `Published` for all new release files.

The updated method looks like this:

```csharp
  private async Task UpdateReleaseFilesPublishedDate(ReleaseVersion releaseVersion, DateTimeOffset publishedDate)
  {
      // Only update the Published date for new or updated files, i.e. where the Published date is null.
      // This is to preserve the original published date for unchanged files on an amendment version.
      var newOrUpdatedReleaseFiles = await contentDbContext
          .ReleaseFiles.Where(rf => rf.ReleaseVersionId == releaseVersion.Id)
          .Where(rf => rf.Published == null)
          .ToListAsync();

      newOrUpdatedReleaseFiles.ForEach(rf => rf.Published = publishedDate.UtcDateTime);
  }
```

and the value passed to this method is now `actualPublishedDate` rather than `publishedDisplayDate`.

